1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,661
Welcome to Module 4.

2
00:00:03,011 --> 00:00:07,306
This is the module where we
build our first real AI agent.

3
00:00:07,656 --> 00:00:13,290
The word agent is used for almost
everything these days, so before we write any

4
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:18,212
code, let's get a clear, simple
definition that you can actually use.

5
00:00:18,562 --> 00:00:23,852
By the end of this module, you'll have
built a smart calculator agent from scratch,

6
00:00:23,852 --> 00:00:26,338
and you'll understand every line of it.

7
00:00:28,138 --> 00:00:33,305
In this lesson, you'll learn to tell a
chatbot, a workflow, and an agent apart.

8
00:00:33,905 --> 00:00:37,694
You'll be able to explain who
decides the next step in each one.

9
00:00:38,294 --> 00:00:43,745
And you'll learn to choose the simplest
design that does the job, which is often not

10
00:00:43,745 --> 00:00:44,329
an agent.

11
00:00:46,129 --> 00:00:48,065
Let's start with a quick look back.

12
00:00:48,415 --> 00:00:54,261
In Module 2, you learned to send a
request to a language model and read its reply.

13
00:00:54,860 --> 00:01:00,791
In Module 3, you got validated data
back, and you split a bigger task into smaller

14
00:01:00,791 --> 00:01:01,225
steps.

15
00:01:01,825 --> 00:01:03,629
Now we add one new idea.

16
00:01:03,979 --> 00:01:09,105
Instead of your code choosing every
step, we let the model choose the next step

17
00:01:09,105 --> 00:01:09,560
itself.

18
00:01:09,910 --> 00:01:13,762
That one change is what
turns a program into an agent.

19
00:01:15,562 --> 00:01:19,113
There are three common ways
to build with a language model.

20
00:01:19,463 --> 00:01:23,668
The difference between them is
simple: who decides what happens next?

21
00:01:24,268 --> 00:01:25,529
First, a chatbot.

22
00:01:25,879 --> 00:01:28,724
You send a message, and the model replies.

23
00:01:29,074 --> 00:01:29,918
That's it.

24
00:01:30,268 --> 00:01:35,201
If something else needs to happen,
you, the human, decide what to ask next.

25
00:01:35,801 --> 00:01:37,166
Second, a workflow.

26
00:01:37,516 --> 00:01:43,141
Your program runs a fixed list of steps,
in a fixed order, and some of those steps

27
00:01:43,141 --> 00:01:44,238
are model calls.

28
00:01:44,588 --> 00:01:51,210
The resume analyzer from Module 3 is a
workflow: extract, compare, advise, always in

29
00:01:51,210 --> 00:01:52,077
that order.

30
00:01:52,677 --> 00:01:53,887
Third, an agent.

31
00:01:54,237 --> 00:01:59,998
The model chooses an action, like using
a tool, sees the result, and then chooses

32
00:01:59,998 --> 00:02:00,425
again.

33
00:02:00,775 --> 00:02:04,122
It repeats this in a
loop until the job is done.

34
00:02:04,472 --> 00:02:07,340
The model decides
the steps, not your code.

35
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:12,499
Here's the picture we'll keep
coming back to in this module.

36
00:02:12,849 --> 00:02:14,170
Take your time with it.

37
00:02:14,770 --> 00:02:17,645
It starts with a goal,
here, a maths question.

38
00:02:17,995 --> 00:02:21,323
The model reads it and
decides what to do first.

39
00:02:21,923 --> 00:02:24,393
Say it decides to use the calculator.

40
00:02:24,743 --> 00:02:28,639
Important: the model
can't run the calculator itself.

41
00:02:28,989 --> 00:02:30,353
It can only ask.

42
00:02:30,703 --> 00:02:33,066
Your program does the actual work.

43
00:02:33,416 --> 00:02:34,703
That's the act step.

44
00:02:35,303 --> 00:02:37,354
The result goes back to the model.

45
00:02:37,704 --> 00:02:39,137
That's the observe step.

46
00:02:39,487 --> 00:02:43,001
Now the model knows
something new, so it decides again.

47
00:02:43,351 --> 00:02:45,937
Maybe it needs another calculation.

48
00:02:46,537 --> 00:02:51,707
And when the model decides nothing is
left to do, it answers, and the loop ends.

49
00:02:52,057 --> 00:02:54,116
Decide, act, observe, repeat.

50
00:02:54,466 --> 00:02:57,769
That loop is the heart of
every agent in this course.

51
00:02:59,569 --> 00:03:01,206
So when should you use which?

52
00:03:01,556 --> 00:03:05,396
Ask yourself one question:
do I know the steps in advance?

53
00:03:05,746 --> 00:03:07,609
If you do, use a workflow.

54
00:03:07,959 --> 00:03:13,348
It's predictable, its cost is
predictable, and you can test each step on its own.

55
00:03:13,948 --> 00:03:18,541
If the steps depend on what you
discover along the way, use an agent.

56
00:03:18,891 --> 00:03:24,849
An agent is flexible, but you pay for
that: its cost and time vary from run to run,

57
00:03:24,849 --> 00:03:26,572
and it's harder to test.

58
00:03:28,372 --> 00:03:32,605
A very practical rule: always
start with the simplest design.

59
00:03:32,955 --> 00:03:36,710
If one request gives you
the answer, use a single call.

60
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:39,894
If the steps are fixed, use a workflow.

61
00:03:40,494 --> 00:03:43,792
Reach for an agent only
when the path really is unknown.

62
00:03:44,392 --> 00:03:50,341
And whatever you choose, actions that are
expensive or can't be undone, like paying,

63
00:03:50,341 --> 00:03:53,528
deleting, or sending,
need checks and limits.

64
00:03:53,878 --> 00:03:56,509
We'll build those
habits from this module on.

65
00:03:58,309 --> 00:04:00,187
Time to check your understanding.

66
00:04:00,537 --> 00:04:06,344
Pause the video, and for each of these
three tasks, decide: single call, workflow,

67
00:04:06,344 --> 00:04:06,981
or agent?

68
00:04:07,581 --> 00:04:08,219
One.

69
00:04:08,569 --> 00:04:10,766
Translate a paragraph into Spanish.

70
00:04:11,366 --> 00:04:11,987
Two.

71
00:04:12,337 --> 00:04:16,652
Turn a resume and a job
posting into a skills-gap report.

72
00:04:17,252 --> 00:04:17,949
Three.

73
00:04:18,299 --> 00:04:22,030
Plan a trip within a
budget, checking prices as you go.

74
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:29,011
Here are my answers.

75
00:04:29,361 --> 00:04:33,325
Translation is a single
call: one request, one answer.

76
00:04:33,675 --> 00:04:37,896
The gap report is a workflow:
the steps are always the same.

77
00:04:38,246 --> 00:04:43,757
And trip planning is an agent: what to
check next depends on the prices you find.

78
00:04:45,557 --> 00:04:46,416
Quick check.

79
00:04:46,766 --> 00:04:48,650
What makes a program an agent?

80
00:04:54,850 --> 00:04:58,683
The model decides the next
step, in a loop, using tools.

81
00:04:59,033 --> 00:05:02,640
In a workflow, your code
makes those decisions instead.

82
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:07,159
A chatbot answers one question at a time.

83
00:05:07,759 --> 00:05:10,627
In a workflow, your
code decides the steps.

84
00:05:11,227 --> 00:05:15,495
In an agent, the model
decides, acts, observes, and repeats.

85
00:05:16,095 --> 00:05:19,555
And you should always choose
the simplest design that works.

86
00:05:20,155 --> 00:05:25,158
Next lesson, we open an agent up and
look at its four parts: the model, the

87
00:05:25,158 --> 00:05:27,759
instructions, the tools, and the state.

88
00:05:29,561 --> 00:05:34,824
In the last lesson you saw the
agent loop: decide, act, observe, repeat.

89
00:05:35,174 --> 00:05:37,110
Now let's open an agent up.

90
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:43,181
Every agent, from a tiny calculator to
a big coding assistant, is built from the

91
00:05:43,181 --> 00:05:44,325
same four parts.

92
00:05:44,675 --> 00:05:49,413
Once you can name them, any agent
becomes much easier to understand.

93
00:05:51,213 --> 00:05:54,631
In this lesson, you'll
name the four parts of an agent.

94
00:05:55,231 --> 00:06:00,340
You'll understand what a tool is, and,
very importantly, who actually runs it.

95
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:04,343
You'll read a tool
definition, and a real tool call.

96
00:06:04,943 --> 00:06:07,338
And you'll see where
the agent keeps its state.

97
00:06:09,138 --> 00:06:10,475
Part one: the model.

98
00:06:10,825 --> 00:06:14,703
It reads everything so far
and decides the next action.

99
00:06:15,053 --> 00:06:16,536
It's the decision maker.

100
00:06:17,136 --> 00:06:18,838
Part two: the instructions.

101
00:06:19,188 --> 00:06:21,970
That's the system
prompt you know from Module 2.

102
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:25,622
It sets the goal, the
rules, and the style of the answer.

103
00:06:26,222 --> 00:06:27,767
Part three: the tools.

104
00:06:28,116 --> 00:06:32,521
These are functions in your
program that the model can ask you to run.

105
00:06:32,871 --> 00:06:35,669
Without tools, a model can only talk.

106
00:06:36,269 --> 00:06:37,863
And part four: the state.

107
00:06:38,213 --> 00:06:43,699
That's the list of messages: the
question, every action the model took, and every

108
00:06:43,699 --> 00:06:44,986
result it got back.

109
00:06:46,786 --> 00:06:52,767
Let's slow down on tools, because this
is the idea most beginners find surprising.

110
00:06:53,117 --> 00:06:55,783
A language model can only produce text.

111
00:06:56,133 --> 00:07:01,946
It can't run code, it can't open your
files, and it can't browse the web by itself.

112
00:07:02,546 --> 00:07:06,751
A tool is just a normal
Python function, in your program.

113
00:07:07,101 --> 00:07:10,637
For our calculator, it's
a function called calculate.

114
00:07:11,237 --> 00:07:16,937
You describe that function to the model:
its name, what it does, and what inputs it

115
00:07:16,937 --> 00:07:17,349
needs.

116
00:07:17,949 --> 00:07:23,369
Then the model can ask for the tool to
be called, and your code decides to run it,

117
00:07:23,369 --> 00:07:25,087
and sends back the result.

118
00:07:25,437 --> 00:07:28,451
The model never
touches your computer directly.

119
00:07:28,801 --> 00:07:30,247
You stay in control.

120
00:07:32,047 --> 00:07:35,506
Here's how we describe the
calculate tool to the model.

121
00:07:35,856 --> 00:07:36,932
First, a name.

122
00:07:37,282 --> 00:07:40,552
The model uses this name
when it asks for the tool.

123
00:07:41,152 --> 00:07:43,576
Then a description, written for the model.

124
00:07:43,926 --> 00:07:49,126
It says what the tool does, what it
accepts, what it returns, and what happens when

125
00:07:49,126 --> 00:07:50,442
something goes wrong.

126
00:07:50,792 --> 00:07:56,489
A clear description is the main way the
model knows when, and how, to use a tool.

127
00:07:57,089 --> 00:07:58,563
Then the input schema.

128
00:07:58,913 --> 00:08:01,099
You've seen schemas in Module 3.

129
00:08:01,449 --> 00:08:06,344
This one says the tool takes one
input, called expression, and it's a string.

130
00:08:06,694 --> 00:08:09,684
It's required, and no
other inputs are allowed.

131
00:08:10,284 --> 00:08:16,144
And strict true asks the API to make
sure the model's input matches this schema

132
00:08:16,144 --> 00:08:16,738
exactly.

133
00:08:18,538 --> 00:08:22,548
Now let's see what comes back
when the model wants to use a tool.

134
00:08:22,898 --> 00:08:27,348
This script sends one question,
with the tool definitions attached.

135
00:08:27,948 --> 00:08:33,749
Remember, this recording uses the
offline fake API, so a script plays the model's

136
00:08:33,749 --> 00:08:34,107
part.

137
00:08:34,457 --> 00:08:38,789
The shape of the reply is
exactly what the real API sends.

138
00:08:39,139 --> 00:08:44,120
The stop reason is tool use: the
model stopped because it wants a tool.

139
00:08:44,470 --> 00:08:50,112
The block's type is tool use, the name
is calculate, the input is the expression,

140
00:08:50,112 --> 00:08:53,455
and there's an ID,
which we'll need in a moment.

141
00:08:55,255 --> 00:08:56,847
So here's the full picture.

142
00:08:57,197 --> 00:09:01,843
The model writes a request: please
call calculate with this expression.

143
00:09:02,193 --> 00:09:03,387
That's all it does.

144
00:09:03,987 --> 00:09:08,484
Your program reads that request
and runs the real Python function.

145
00:09:08,834 --> 00:09:12,415
This is where you can check
the request first, or refuse it.

146
00:09:13,015 --> 00:09:17,531
And your program sends the result
back to the model, so it can continue.

147
00:09:19,331 --> 00:09:20,958
Part two, the instructions.

148
00:09:21,308 --> 00:09:24,142
Notice we use everything from Module 3.

149
00:09:24,492 --> 00:09:27,396
First, context: who
this helps, and with what.

150
00:09:27,996 --> 00:09:33,360
Then the rules, each with a reason:
work numbers out with the tools, because the

151
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:34,433
tools are exact.

152
00:09:34,783 --> 00:09:39,025
Models can make arithmetic
mistakes, but a calculator can't.

153
00:09:39,375 --> 00:09:43,663
And if a tool returns an
error, fix the input and try again.

154
00:09:44,263 --> 00:09:49,325
Finally, the shape of the answer: one
or two sentences, with the number and its

155
00:09:49,325 --> 00:09:49,645
unit.

156
00:09:51,445 --> 00:09:52,966
And part four, the state.

157
00:09:53,316 --> 00:09:58,402
Remember from Module 2: the API
remembers nothing between requests.

158
00:09:59,002 --> 00:10:04,297
So the agent keeps a list of messages:
the question, each tool request from the

159
00:10:04,297 --> 00:10:07,046
model, and each result from your program.

160
00:10:07,646 --> 00:10:12,955
Every time the agent asks the model
to decide, it sends the whole list again.

161
00:10:13,305 --> 00:10:16,353
That's how the model
knows what has already happened.

162
00:10:16,953 --> 00:10:22,080
This means the list grows every
turn, and so does the cost of each request.

163
00:10:22,430 --> 00:10:26,315
That's one reason agents
need limits, which is lesson four.

164
00:10:28,115 --> 00:10:28,995
Your turn.

165
00:10:29,345 --> 00:10:31,325
Imagine a tool called get weather.

166
00:10:31,675 --> 00:10:35,397
Pause the video, and write down
what its description should say.

167
00:10:35,997 --> 00:10:40,971
Think about what it does, what inputs
it needs, what it returns, and how it can

168
00:10:40,971 --> 00:10:41,286
fail.

169
00:10:46,886 --> 00:10:51,808
A good answer: it returns the current
temperature and conditions for a city.

170
00:10:52,158 --> 00:10:53,562
It needs a city name.

171
00:10:53,912 --> 00:10:58,036
It returns the temperature in
Celsius and a short description.

172
00:10:58,386 --> 00:11:01,366
And it returns an error
if the city isn't found.

173
00:11:01,716 --> 00:11:05,232
Notice that's the same
pattern as our calculate tool.

174
00:11:07,032 --> 00:11:07,891
Quick check.

175
00:11:08,241 --> 00:11:13,413
When the agent uses the calculate
tool, who actually runs the calculation?

176
00:11:19,613 --> 00:11:22,737
Your program does,
after the model asks for it.

177
00:11:23,087 --> 00:11:25,312
The model only writes the request.

178
00:11:27,112 --> 00:11:32,216
An agent has four parts: the model,
the instructions, the tools, and the state.

179
00:11:32,816 --> 00:11:34,370
A tool is your function.

180
00:11:34,720 --> 00:11:36,535
The model only asks for it.

181
00:11:37,135 --> 00:11:41,660
A tool definition has a name, a
description, and an input schema.

182
00:11:42,260 --> 00:11:46,290
And the state is the message
list, sent in full every turn.

183
00:11:46,890 --> 00:11:52,524
Next lesson, we connect the four parts
and write the agent loop itself, in about

184
00:11:52,524 --> 00:11:54,144
twenty lines of Python.

185
00:11:55,950 --> 00:11:58,074
You know the four parts of an agent.

186
00:11:58,424 --> 00:11:59,692
Now we connect them.

187
00:12:00,042 --> 00:12:05,205
In this lesson we write the agent
loop itself: about twenty lines of Python.

188
00:12:05,555 --> 00:12:11,323
We'll go slowly, one small piece at a
time, and then run it and look at exactly what

189
00:12:11,323 --> 00:12:11,941
happened.

190
00:12:13,741 --> 00:12:17,488
You'll write a loop that
decides, acts, observes, and stops.

191
00:12:18,088 --> 00:12:22,253
You'll send tool results back
in the format the API expects.

192
00:12:22,853 --> 00:12:26,708
And you'll follow how the
message list grows, turn by turn.

193
00:12:28,508 --> 00:12:33,906
We start with a client, the tool
definitions from our project, and the message list.

194
00:12:34,256 --> 00:12:39,945
Right now the list holds just one
message: the user's question, which needs two

195
00:12:39,945 --> 00:12:40,881
calculations.

196
00:12:41,481 --> 00:12:42,482
Then the loop.

197
00:12:42,832 --> 00:12:47,951
Notice it's a for loop that runs at
most five times, not a while true loop.

198
00:12:48,301 --> 00:12:52,179
If something goes wrong,
this agent can never run forever.

199
00:12:52,529 --> 00:12:54,211
Always give a loop a limit.

200
00:12:54,811 --> 00:12:56,170
Step one, decide.

201
00:12:56,520 --> 00:13:01,825
We send the tools and the whole message
list to the model, and it decides what to do

202
00:13:01,825 --> 00:13:02,141
next.

203
00:13:02,741 --> 00:13:07,100
We add the model's reply to
the list, exactly as it came back.

204
00:13:07,450 --> 00:13:09,519
Don't edit it, and don't rebuild it.

205
00:13:09,869 --> 00:13:12,761
The next request
must contain it unchanged.

206
00:13:13,361 --> 00:13:16,122
Now we look for tool
requests in the reply.

207
00:13:16,472 --> 00:13:21,044
If there aren't any, the model has
finished, so we print its answer and stop.

208
00:13:21,394 --> 00:13:22,898
That's step four, stop.

209
00:13:23,498 --> 00:13:25,222
Otherwise, step two, act.

210
00:13:25,572 --> 00:13:31,304
For each tool request, our code runs
the tool, using the name and the input the

211
00:13:31,304 --> 00:13:32,320
model gave us.

212
00:13:32,920 --> 00:13:34,454
Step three, observe.

213
00:13:34,804 --> 00:13:37,250
We wrap each output in a tool result.

214
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:41,888
The tool use ID connects the
result to the request it answers.

215
00:13:42,238 --> 00:13:44,640
That's why every request has an ID.

216
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:48,487
And we add the results to
the list as a user message.

217
00:13:48,837 --> 00:13:53,746
Then the loop goes round again, and
the model decides with the new information.

218
00:13:55,546 --> 00:13:56,484
Let's run it.

219
00:13:56,834 --> 00:14:02,539
Again, the offline fake plays the model,
following a fixed script, but the loop is

220
00:14:02,539 --> 00:14:04,697
exactly the code you just read.

221
00:14:05,297 --> 00:14:11,483
Turn one: the model asks for eighteen
times one point one five, and our code returns

222
00:14:11,483 --> 00:14:12,882
twenty point seven.

223
00:14:13,232 --> 00:14:19,450
Turn two: it asks for twenty point seven
divided by three, and gets six point nine.

224
00:14:19,800 --> 00:14:24,271
Turn three: no more tool
requests, so it answers, and the loop stops.

225
00:14:24,621 --> 00:14:28,811
Offline, that answer is a
placeholder that lists the results.

226
00:14:30,611 --> 00:14:32,712
The script printed six messages.

227
00:14:33,062 --> 00:14:36,978
Let's walk through them,
because this list is the agent's memory.

228
00:14:37,328 --> 00:14:39,020
One: the user's question.

229
00:14:39,620 --> 00:14:41,984
Two: the model's first tool request.

230
00:14:42,584 --> 00:14:45,747
Three: our result, sent as a user message.

231
00:14:46,347 --> 00:14:48,386
Four: the second tool request.

232
00:14:48,986 --> 00:14:50,722
Five: the second result.

233
00:14:51,322 --> 00:14:53,257
And six: the final answer.

234
00:14:53,607 --> 00:14:58,098
Three requests in total, and
each one sent the whole list so far.

235
00:14:59,898 --> 00:15:02,839
When you build a loop
like this, three rules matter.

236
00:15:03,189 --> 00:15:06,755
First, tool results
always go back in a user message.

237
00:15:07,355 --> 00:15:11,889
Second, every tool request
needs a result with the same ID.

238
00:15:12,239 --> 00:15:16,635
If the model asks for two
tools at once, send back two results.

239
00:15:17,235 --> 00:15:20,592
And third, send the
model's replies back unchanged.

240
00:15:20,942 --> 00:15:26,079
Current models check that the history
hasn't been edited, so treat the list as

241
00:15:26,079 --> 00:15:30,228
append only: you add to the
end, and never change what's there.

242
00:15:32,028 --> 00:15:33,751
Pause the video and predict.

243
00:15:34,101 --> 00:15:39,553
A question needs three separate
calculations, and the model asks for one per turn.

244
00:15:39,903 --> 00:15:41,956
How many requests will the loop make?

245
00:15:42,556 --> 00:15:45,817
And how many messages
will the list hold at the end?

246
00:15:51,417 --> 00:15:55,388
Four requests: three tool
turns, plus one for the answer.

247
00:15:55,738 --> 00:16:01,335
And eight messages: the question, then
three pairs of request and result, then the

248
00:16:01,335 --> 00:16:01,813
answer.

249
00:16:03,613 --> 00:16:04,472
Quick check.

250
00:16:04,822 --> 00:16:07,343
How does the loop know
the agent has finished?

251
00:16:13,543 --> 00:16:16,156
The reply contains no tool requests.

252
00:16:16,506 --> 00:16:21,164
The model chose to answer
instead, and the stop reason is end turn.

253
00:16:22,964 --> 00:16:27,533
The loop decides, acts,
observes, and repeats, always with a limit.

254
00:16:28,133 --> 00:16:31,470
No tool requests means
the model has answered.

255
00:16:32,070 --> 00:16:35,916
Results go back as user
messages, matched by ID.

256
00:16:36,516 --> 00:16:38,475
And the history is append only.

257
00:16:39,075 --> 00:16:44,088
Next lesson: everything that can go
wrong in a loop, and how to stop safely.

258
00:16:45,891 --> 00:16:49,198
A loop that works on a
good day is the easy part.

259
00:16:49,548 --> 00:16:55,303
A good agent also behaves well on a
bad day: when it can't finish, when a tool

260
00:16:55,303 --> 00:16:58,106
fails, or when the input is dangerous.

261
00:16:58,456 --> 00:17:00,958
This lesson is about stopping safely.

262
00:17:01,308 --> 00:17:05,164
These habits will protect
every agent you build from now on.

263
00:17:06,964 --> 00:17:09,692
You'll list every way
an agent loop can end.

264
00:17:10,292 --> 00:17:14,073
You'll return tool errors to
the model, instead of crashing.

265
00:17:14,673 --> 00:17:17,840
And you'll build tools
that refuse dangerous input.

266
00:17:19,640 --> 00:17:22,948
A well-built agent
knows exactly why it stopped.

267
00:17:23,298 --> 00:17:26,966
Here are the six ways
our calculator agent can end.

268
00:17:27,566 --> 00:17:30,390
First, the good
ending: the model answered.

269
00:17:30,990 --> 00:17:32,348
Second, the step limit.

270
00:17:32,698 --> 00:17:37,844
After a fixed number of turns, we
stop, even if the model wants to continue.

271
00:17:38,444 --> 00:17:40,806
Third, the budget, from Module 2.

272
00:17:41,156 --> 00:17:43,708
When spending reaches the limit, we stop.

273
00:17:44,308 --> 00:17:47,986
Fourth, a refusal: the
model declined the request.

274
00:17:48,586 --> 00:17:51,453
Fifth, the reply was
cut off at max tokens.

275
00:17:52,053 --> 00:17:57,329
And sixth, an API error that
retries couldn't fix, like a rejected key.

276
00:17:57,929 --> 00:18:01,176
For each ending, the
user gets one clear message.

277
00:18:01,526 --> 00:18:04,519
Never a traceback,
and never a silent stop.

278
00:18:06,319 --> 00:18:08,636
Why does the step limit matter so much?

279
00:18:08,986 --> 00:18:12,858
A model can get stuck,
asking for tools again and again.

280
00:18:13,208 --> 00:18:18,741
Our fake API has a mode that does
exactly that, so we can watch the limit work.

281
00:18:19,341 --> 00:18:21,813
The fake keeps asking for one plus one.

282
00:18:22,163 --> 00:18:27,891
After three turns, the agent stops and
explains why, instead of running, and costing

283
00:18:27,891 --> 00:18:28,914
money, forever.

284
00:18:30,714 --> 00:18:32,141
Next, tool errors.

285
00:18:32,491 --> 00:18:38,865
Tools will fail sometimes: the input is
invalid, there's a division by zero, or a

286
00:18:38,865 --> 00:18:40,360
unit doesn't exist.

287
00:18:40,960 --> 00:18:42,913
Don't let that crash your program.

288
00:18:43,263 --> 00:18:47,772
Instead, send the error message
back to the model, as the tool's result.

289
00:18:48,372 --> 00:18:52,469
Mark it with is error, true, so
the model knows the call failed.

290
00:18:53,069 --> 00:18:57,507
Now the model can read the
error, fix its input, and try again.

291
00:18:57,857 --> 00:19:01,236
And your step limit still
caps how many times it can try.

292
00:19:03,036 --> 00:19:04,860
Here's how the project does it.

293
00:19:05,210 --> 00:19:08,568
Act runs one tool call
and records it as a step.

294
00:19:09,168 --> 00:19:12,383
If the tool raises a
tool error, we don't crash.

295
00:19:12,733 --> 00:19:18,253
The error message becomes the
observation, marked as an error, and the loop carries

296
00:19:18,253 --> 00:19:18,452
on.

297
00:19:20,252 --> 00:19:26,289
Here the fake is scripted to send a
broken expression first: eighteen x one point

298
00:19:26,289 --> 00:19:26,960
one five.

299
00:19:27,310 --> 00:19:31,650
The calculator returns an
error, shown with an exclamation mark.

300
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:36,517
On the next turn the expression is
fixed, and the agent finishes normally.

301
00:19:36,867 --> 00:19:40,119
With a real model, this
recovery isn't scripted.

302
00:19:40,469 --> 00:19:45,801
The model reads the error message and
corrects itself, which is why clear error

303
00:19:45,801 --> 00:19:46,881
messages matter.

304
00:19:48,681 --> 00:19:51,786
Now the most important
safety rule in this lesson.

305
00:19:52,136 --> 00:19:57,637
Tool input comes from the model, and
the model's input comes, partly, from users.

306
00:19:57,987 --> 00:20:01,209
So treat tool input
like any untrusted input.

307
00:20:01,559 --> 00:20:07,531
A calculator built with Python's eval
function looks clever, but eval runs any

308
00:20:07,531 --> 00:20:10,594
Python code at all, not just arithmetic.

309
00:20:11,194 --> 00:20:16,792
Instead, our calculator parses the
expression into a tree, and walks through it,

310
00:20:16,792 --> 00:20:20,991
allowing only numbers, a few
operators, and three functions.

311
00:20:21,341 --> 00:20:23,096
Anything else is refused.

312
00:20:24,896 --> 00:20:28,549
This script shows the
difference, with a harmless example.

313
00:20:28,899 --> 00:20:34,845
Given this text, eval happily imports
the os module and runs a function: here it

314
00:20:34,845 --> 00:20:36,778
prints the current folder.

315
00:20:37,128 --> 00:20:39,769
It could just as easily delete files.

316
00:20:40,119 --> 00:20:45,124
Our calculate function refuses it,
and still does real maths perfectly.

317
00:20:46,924 --> 00:20:48,141
Here's the idea.

318
00:20:48,491 --> 00:20:52,259
Each kind of node in the
tree must be explicitly allowed.

319
00:20:52,609 --> 00:20:56,178
Numbers are allowed, and
so are plus and minus signs.

320
00:20:56,778 --> 00:21:02,604
Arithmetic operators are allowed, but
only the ones in our list, and very large

321
00:21:02,604 --> 00:21:08,430
powers are refused, so nobody can
freeze your program with a giant calculation.

322
00:21:09,030 --> 00:21:14,016
Three named functions are allowed:
square root, absolute value, and round.

323
00:21:14,616 --> 00:21:16,466
Everything else is refused.

324
00:21:16,816 --> 00:21:21,015
That's an allow list: you list
what's permitted, and refuse the rest.

325
00:21:21,365 --> 00:21:24,974
It's much safer than trying to
list everything that's dangerous.

326
00:21:26,774 --> 00:21:27,837
One more thing.

327
00:21:28,187 --> 00:21:34,039
In real projects, the Anthropic SDK has
a tool runner that can run this loop for

328
00:21:34,039 --> 00:21:34,331
you.

329
00:21:34,931 --> 00:21:40,115
We built the loop by hand, so you
understand exactly what any tool runner does

330
00:21:40,115 --> 00:21:40,846
underneath.

331
00:21:41,446 --> 00:21:47,496
In Module 5, you'll add more tools,
validate their inputs, and ask a human for

332
00:21:47,496 --> 00:21:50,133
confirmation before risky actions.

333
00:21:50,733 --> 00:21:56,438
And in Module 7, you'll make agents
reliable, with timeouts, token budgets, logging,

334
00:21:56,438 --> 00:21:57,252
and tracing.

335
00:21:59,052 --> 00:22:02,992
Pause and match each problem
to the protection that handles it.

336
00:22:03,592 --> 00:22:04,231
One.

337
00:22:04,581 --> 00:22:08,188
The model keeps asking
for tools, and never answers.

338
00:22:08,788 --> 00:22:09,410
Two.

339
00:22:09,760 --> 00:22:13,412
A user tries to sneak
Python code into the calculator.

340
00:22:14,012 --> 00:22:14,709
Three.

341
00:22:15,059 --> 00:22:19,206
The model sends a letter x
instead of a multiplication sign.

342
00:22:24,806 --> 00:22:26,146
One: the step limit.

343
00:22:26,496 --> 00:22:31,831
Two: the allow-list calculator, which
refuses anything that isn't arithmetic.

344
00:22:32,181 --> 00:22:36,465
Three: the tool error goes back
to the model, which fixes its input.

345
00:22:38,265 --> 00:22:39,124
Quick check.

346
00:22:39,474 --> 00:22:44,180
Why send a tool's error back to the
model, instead of raising an exception?

347
00:22:50,380 --> 00:22:54,080
So the model can read it,
fix its input, and continue.

348
00:22:54,430 --> 00:22:57,453
And the step limit still
caps how often it can try.

349
00:22:59,253 --> 00:23:03,415
A loop can end six ways,
and each gets one clear message.

350
00:23:04,015 --> 00:23:06,168
A step limit stops endless loops.

351
00:23:06,768 --> 00:23:10,438
Tool errors are
observations, marked with is error.

352
00:23:11,038 --> 00:23:13,005
And never eval tool input.

353
00:23:13,355 --> 00:23:14,753
Use an allow list.

354
00:23:15,353 --> 00:23:18,277
You now know every idea the project needs.

355
00:23:18,627 --> 00:23:22,256
Next lesson, we build
the smart calculator agent.

356
00:23:24,078 --> 00:23:25,236
Project time.

357
00:23:25,586 --> 00:23:31,831
We're building a smart calculator agent:
you ask an everyday maths question, and the

358
00:23:31,831 --> 00:23:36,811
agent works it out with real
tools, step by step, showing its work.

359
00:23:37,161 --> 00:23:40,572
Everything from the last
four lessons comes together here.

360
00:23:40,922 --> 00:23:42,966
We'll take it one file at a time.

361
00:23:44,766 --> 00:23:46,178
Here's what we're building.

362
00:23:46,528 --> 00:23:50,528
You ask a maths question in
plain words, at the command line.

363
00:23:51,128 --> 00:23:56,950
The agent has two tools: calculate,
for arithmetic, and convert, for units like

364
00:23:56,950 --> 00:23:58,424
kilometres to miles.

365
00:23:59,024 --> 00:24:02,532
It prints every action
and every result as it goes.

366
00:24:02,882 --> 00:24:08,113
That printout is called a trace, and it
lets you see exactly what the agent did.

367
00:24:08,713 --> 00:24:14,399
And it has a step limit, a budget, clear
errors, an offline mode, and a full set of

368
00:24:14,399 --> 00:24:14,810
tests.

369
00:24:16,610 --> 00:24:17,740
Here's the plan.

370
00:24:18,090 --> 00:24:22,673
The command line passes your
question to the agent, which runs the loop.

371
00:24:23,273 --> 00:24:29,309
The agent runs tools from tools dot py,
and adds up costs with the pricing module

372
00:24:29,309 --> 00:24:30,352
from Module 2.

373
00:24:30,952 --> 00:24:36,852
And it talks to the model through the
client: the real Claude API with a key, or the

374
00:24:36,852 --> 00:24:38,608
offline fake without one.

375
00:24:40,408 --> 00:24:45,718
First, a word about the offline fake, so
you know exactly what you're seeing in this

376
00:24:45,718 --> 00:24:46,097
video.

377
00:24:46,447 --> 00:24:53,174
It speaks the same protocol as the real
API, so the SDK and our loop run unchanged.

378
00:24:53,524 --> 00:24:59,142
But it follows a fixed script: it
finds arithmetic and unit conversions in the

379
00:24:59,142 --> 00:25:04,256
question, asks for one tool per
turn, then writes a placeholder answer.

380
00:25:04,856 --> 00:25:06,110
That's not a model.

381
00:25:06,460 --> 00:25:11,563
It can't understand a word problem, it
can't plan, and it can't check its work.

382
00:25:11,913 --> 00:25:17,700
No API key was used in this recording,
so every model turn you see comes from this

383
00:25:17,700 --> 00:25:18,194
script.

384
00:25:18,544 --> 00:25:21,677
The tools and the loop,
though, are completely real.

385
00:25:23,477 --> 00:25:24,910
You've seen calculate.

386
00:25:25,260 --> 00:25:27,013
The second tool is convert.

387
00:25:27,363 --> 00:25:33,117
It uses one table: each unit has a
kind, like length or mass, and a factor that

388
00:25:33,117 --> 00:25:35,083
converts it to a base unit.

389
00:25:35,433 --> 00:25:38,940
For example, one
kilometre is a thousand metres.

390
00:25:39,540 --> 00:25:44,911
Temperatures are different: Celsius to
Fahrenheit needs a formula, not a simple

391
00:25:44,911 --> 00:25:47,834
factor, so they get
their own small branch.

392
00:25:48,434 --> 00:25:54,482
An unknown unit, or converting kilograms
to kilometres, raises a tool error with a

393
00:25:54,482 --> 00:25:59,203
helpful message, which goes
back to the model as an observation.

394
00:25:59,803 --> 00:26:05,895
And the conversion itself is one line:
multiply by one factor, divide by the other.

395
00:26:07,695 --> 00:26:12,724
When the model asks for a tool, run
tool connects the name to the right Python

396
00:26:12,724 --> 00:26:13,304
function.

397
00:26:13,654 --> 00:26:15,285
This is called dispatching.

398
00:26:15,885 --> 00:26:18,364
For convert, it also checks the inputs.

399
00:26:18,714 --> 00:26:24,440
If anything is missing or the wrong type,
the model gets a clear message saying what

400
00:26:24,440 --> 00:26:25,326
was expected.

401
00:26:25,926 --> 00:26:30,271
And if the model asks for a tool
that doesn't exist, it's refused.

402
00:26:30,621 --> 00:26:34,007
The agent can only use the
tools you chose to give it.

403
00:26:35,807 --> 00:26:37,393
Now the agent itself.

404
00:26:37,743 --> 00:26:43,258
A step records one action and what was
observed: the tool, its input, its output,

405
00:26:43,258 --> 00:26:45,165
and whether it was an error.

406
00:26:45,765 --> 00:26:51,448
A result holds the answer, and, just as
important, why the loop stopped: answered,

407
00:26:51,448 --> 00:26:54,151
max steps, refused, cut off, or budget.

408
00:26:54,501 --> 00:26:58,325
It also keeps the steps, the
number of turns, and the cost.

409
00:27:00,125 --> 00:27:06,016
Decide sends one request: the
instructions, the tools, low effort because these are

410
00:27:06,016 --> 00:27:09,210
simple decisions, and
the whole message list.

411
00:27:09,810 --> 00:27:14,296
Then it records the cost of
that request, and returns the reply.

412
00:27:16,097 --> 00:27:17,235
And here's run.

413
00:27:17,585 --> 00:27:22,281
It's the same loop as lesson three,
with all the safety from lesson four.

414
00:27:22,631 --> 00:27:27,741
It runs at most max steps turns,
and checks the budget before every turn.

415
00:27:28,341 --> 00:27:34,637
Decide, store the reply unchanged,
then stop cleanly on a refusal or a cut-off

416
00:27:34,637 --> 00:27:35,122
reply.

417
00:27:35,722 --> 00:27:38,457
No tool requests means we have the answer.

418
00:27:39,057 --> 00:27:44,611
Otherwise we act, report each step so
the trace can print it, and send back the

419
00:27:44,611 --> 00:27:47,212
results, including the is error flag.

420
00:27:47,812 --> 00:27:51,688
And if the loop runs out of
turns, we stop with max steps.

421
00:27:53,488 --> 00:27:58,944
First, the calc command runs the
calculator tool directly, with no model at all.

422
00:27:59,294 --> 00:28:02,445
It's useful for checking
that a tool works on its own.

423
00:28:03,045 --> 00:28:04,358
And now the agent.

424
00:28:04,708 --> 00:28:08,127
One act, one observe,
and an answer, in two turns.

425
00:28:08,477 --> 00:28:13,603
The footer shows the turns, the tool
calls, and the cost, which is an estimate

426
00:28:13,603 --> 00:28:14,128
offline.

427
00:28:15,928 --> 00:28:19,881
Pause and think it through,
using the run tool code you saw.

428
00:28:20,481 --> 00:28:21,120
One.

429
00:28:21,470 --> 00:28:24,472
The model asks for a
tool called delete files.

430
00:28:24,822 --> 00:28:25,838
What happens?

431
00:28:26,438 --> 00:28:27,060
Two.

432
00:28:27,410 --> 00:28:29,877
It calls convert, with the value lots.

433
00:28:30,227 --> 00:28:31,243
What happens?

434
00:28:36,843 --> 00:28:43,267
One: run tool raises a tool error,
unknown tool, which goes back to the model as an

435
00:28:43,267 --> 00:28:44,196
observation.

436
00:28:44,546 --> 00:28:49,482
Nothing is deleted, because that
tool simply doesn't exist in our program.

437
00:28:49,832 --> 00:28:55,750
Two: turning lots into a number fails,
so the model gets a clear message saying

438
00:28:55,750 --> 00:28:59,494
convert needs a value, a
from unit, and a to unit.

439
00:29:01,294 --> 00:29:05,491
We built two tools with clear
errors: calculate and convert.

440
00:29:06,091 --> 00:29:10,429
Run tool dispatches by
name, and refuses unknown tools.

441
00:29:11,029 --> 00:29:14,950
And the loop records every
step, and exactly why it stopped.

442
00:29:15,550 --> 00:29:21,543
In part two: the trace, a multi-step
demo, failures on purpose, the tests, and the

443
00:29:21,543 --> 00:29:22,420
module quiz.

444
00:29:24,249 --> 00:29:25,309
Welcome back.

445
00:29:25,659 --> 00:29:26,928
The agent works.

446
00:29:27,278 --> 00:29:32,877
Now we make it easy to watch, run a
bigger demo, break it on purpose, and test it.

447
00:29:33,227 --> 00:29:37,721
Then we'll wrap up the module with
a quiz and your extension challenge.

448
00:29:39,521 --> 00:29:42,615
A trace prints what the
agent does while it works.

449
00:29:42,965 --> 00:29:48,576
For each step: an act line with the tool
and its input, and an observe line with the

450
00:29:48,576 --> 00:29:49,044
result.

451
00:29:49,394 --> 00:29:54,194
An equals sign means success,
an exclamation mark means an error.

452
00:29:54,794 --> 00:30:00,680
Ask builds the agent and runs it, passing
show step as the on step callback, so each

453
00:30:00,680 --> 00:30:03,063
step prints as soon as it happens.

454
00:30:03,663 --> 00:30:09,323
The summary line, with turns, tool
calls, and cost, goes to standard error, so it

455
00:30:09,323 --> 00:30:11,280
never mixes with the answer.

456
00:30:11,880 --> 00:30:17,276
And if the agent stopped for any reason
other than answering, that reason becomes

457
00:30:17,276 --> 00:30:20,207
one clear error
message, with exit code one.

458
00:30:22,007 --> 00:30:27,858
Now a bigger question, with three pieces
of work: a price with a tip, a split three

459
00:30:27,858 --> 00:30:30,608
ways, and a marathon distance in miles.

460
00:30:31,208 --> 00:30:32,917
Read the trace from the top.

461
00:30:33,267 --> 00:30:36,814
Act: calculate eighteen
times one point one five.

462
00:30:37,164 --> 00:30:38,944
Observe: twenty point seven.

463
00:30:39,294 --> 00:30:40,952
Act: divide by three.

464
00:30:41,302 --> 00:30:43,105
Observe: six point nine.

465
00:30:43,455 --> 00:30:47,947
Act: convert forty-two point
one nine five kilometres to miles.

466
00:30:48,297 --> 00:30:50,825
Observe: twenty-six point two miles.

467
00:30:51,175 --> 00:30:53,115
Four turns, three tool calls.

468
00:30:53,465 --> 00:30:57,306
A real model would also
write a friendly final sentence.

469
00:30:57,656 --> 00:31:00,263
Offline, the
placeholder lists the results.

470
00:31:02,063 --> 00:31:04,117
Pause and look at that trace again.

471
00:31:04,717 --> 00:31:09,259
Which tool call would change if the
tip were twenty percent instead of fifteen?

472
00:31:09,859 --> 00:31:12,237
And which other
results would change after it?

473
00:31:17,837 --> 00:31:21,595
The first call changes:
eighteen times one point two.

474
00:31:21,945 --> 00:31:25,285
And so does the split,
because it uses that result.

475
00:31:25,635 --> 00:31:28,541
The marathon
conversion doesn't change at all.

476
00:31:28,891 --> 00:31:32,546
Reading a trace like
this is how you debug agents.

477
00:31:34,346 --> 00:31:36,340
Now let's break things on purpose.

478
00:31:36,690 --> 00:31:39,332
Kilograms to kilometres makes no sense.

479
00:31:39,682 --> 00:31:45,759
The convert tool refuses with a clear
reason, cannot convert mass to length, and the

480
00:31:45,759 --> 00:31:47,278
agent still finishes.

481
00:31:47,628 --> 00:31:51,004
A real model would
explain the problem to the user.

482
00:31:51,604 --> 00:31:54,455
A refusal ends the
loop with one clear line.

483
00:31:55,055 --> 00:31:57,379
And a rejected API key does too.

484
00:31:57,729 --> 00:31:59,336
No tracebacks anywhere.

485
00:32:01,136 --> 00:32:05,433
The tests run the real loop and
the real tools against the fake.

486
00:32:05,783 --> 00:32:11,164
This first one checks the full run:
which tools were used, their outputs, and the

487
00:32:11,164 --> 00:32:12,227
number of turns.

488
00:32:12,827 --> 00:32:18,344
This one checks what was sent: the tools,
the system prompt, and the history growing

489
00:32:18,344 --> 00:32:20,052
from one message to three.

490
00:32:20,652 --> 00:32:26,302
This checks the rule from lesson three:
each tool result carries the same ID as the

491
00:32:26,302 --> 00:32:27,595
request it answers.

492
00:32:28,195 --> 00:32:33,082
And these test the safety from lesson
four: recovering from a tool error, and

493
00:32:33,082 --> 00:32:34,795
stopping at the step limit.

494
00:32:36,595 --> 00:32:40,504
Thirty-four tests, all
passing, with no key and no network.

495
00:32:40,854 --> 00:32:47,111
They include seven dangerous or invalid
expressions that the calculator must refuse.

496
00:32:47,711 --> 00:32:50,291
And mypy is happy with every type hint.

497
00:32:52,091 --> 00:32:55,891
If the agent stops after
too many turns, read the trace.

498
00:32:56,241 --> 00:33:01,475
If it repeats the same call, the tool's
error message probably isn't clear enough.

499
00:33:01,825 --> 00:33:05,625
Only raise the step limit
if the task really needs more.

500
00:33:06,225 --> 00:33:11,293
If the model answers without using the
tools, check that the description and the

501
00:33:11,293 --> 00:33:13,954
instructions say clearly when to use them.

502
00:33:14,554 --> 00:33:21,166
A four hundred error about tool use IDs
means a result is missing, or its ID doesn't

503
00:33:21,166 --> 00:33:21,639
match.

504
00:33:21,989 --> 00:33:25,930
Every request needs its
result, in the next user message.

505
00:33:26,530 --> 00:33:29,156
And placeholder
answers mean you're offline.

506
00:33:29,506 --> 00:33:30,683
That's expected.

507
00:33:31,033 --> 00:33:33,307
Set your API key to use Claude.

508
00:33:35,107 --> 00:33:39,918
Chatbots, workflows, and agents
differ in who decides the next step.

509
00:33:40,518 --> 00:33:45,791
Every agent has four parts: the model,
the instructions, the tools, and the state.

510
00:33:46,391 --> 00:33:50,960
The loop decides, acts,
observes, and repeats, always with a limit.

511
00:33:51,560 --> 00:33:55,718
Tool errors are
observations, and tools use allow lists.

512
00:33:56,318 --> 00:33:58,724
And every ending has one clear reason.

513
00:34:00,524 --> 00:34:02,420
Module quiz, question one.

514
00:34:02,770 --> 00:34:07,878
The resume analyzer always
runs extract, compare, then advise.

515
00:34:08,228 --> 00:34:09,132
What is it?

516
00:34:15,332 --> 00:34:16,317
A workflow.

517
00:34:16,667 --> 00:34:19,392
Its steps are fixed, and chosen by code.

518
00:34:21,192 --> 00:34:22,282
Question two.

519
00:34:22,632 --> 00:34:24,961
What does a stop reason of tool use mean?

520
00:34:31,161 --> 00:34:33,798
The model wants your
program to run a tool.

521
00:34:34,148 --> 00:34:37,290
Your program runs it,
and sends back the result.

522
00:34:39,090 --> 00:34:40,222
Question three.

523
00:34:40,572 --> 00:34:42,268
Where do tool results go?

524
00:34:48,468 --> 00:34:53,887
In the next user message, as tool
result blocks, each with the matching ID.

525
00:34:55,687 --> 00:34:56,801
Question four.

526
00:34:57,151 --> 00:35:00,087
Why give the loop a
limit, instead of while true?

527
00:35:06,287 --> 00:35:12,173
It guarantees the agent stops, even if
the model never finishes, and it limits the

528
00:35:12,173 --> 00:35:12,532
cost.

529
00:35:14,332 --> 00:35:15,696
And question five.

530
00:35:16,046 --> 00:35:20,226
Why does the calculator use
an allow list, instead of eval?

531
00:35:26,426 --> 00:35:29,402
Because eval runs any Python code at all.

532
00:35:29,752 --> 00:35:35,900
Tool input is untrusted, so we allow
only arithmetic, and refuse everything else.

533
00:35:37,700 --> 00:35:39,308
Your extension challenge.

534
00:35:39,658 --> 00:35:44,338
Pick one or more, and write a
test for each, using the fake API.

535
00:35:44,938 --> 00:35:48,830
Add a percent of tool, with
its own definition and errors.

536
00:35:49,430 --> 00:35:53,850
Add a JSON option that prints
the steps and the answer as JSON.

537
00:35:54,450 --> 00:35:59,608
Stop early if the model repeats
exactly the same tool call twice in a row.

538
00:36:00,208 --> 00:36:06,727
Or, with an API key, run five word
problems on a real model, and compare the traces.

539
00:36:08,527 --> 00:36:12,600
These are the official
documentation pages for this module.

540
00:36:12,950 --> 00:36:17,767
Links are in the description, along
with the full source code, and the course

541
00:36:17,767 --> 00:36:18,330
glossary.

542
00:36:23,730 --> 00:36:25,571
That's Module 4 complete.

543
00:36:25,921 --> 00:36:30,751
You've built a real agent from
scratch, and you understand every line of it.

544
00:36:31,101 --> 00:36:37,613
In Module 5, we give agents more tools,
and more responsibility: validating inputs,

545
00:36:37,613 --> 00:36:43,263
handling timeouts, permissions, and
asking a human before risky actions.

546
00:36:43,613 --> 00:36:44,555
See you there.
